.TH AD_WATCH 1 "2026-09-01" "ad 2.0" "ad tools"
.SH NAME
ad_watch \- live-preview how ops transform an old file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ad_watch
<oldfile> <newfile> <opfile> [options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ad_watch
displays three sections in one terminal, refreshing when the op file
changes:
.RS 4
.IP 1. 3
OLD file (with line numbers)
.IP 2. 3
NEW file (with line numbers)
.IP 3. 3
DIFF: new vs (old + ops applied), via
.B diff-so-fancy
if available, else plain
.BR "diff -u" .
.RE
.PP
Uses
.B inotifywait
(inotify-tools) for instant, event-driven refresh. Falls back to
.B stat
mtime polling (0.2s interval) if
.B inotifywait
is not installed.
.PP
The ops are applied using the real animator
.RB ( bin/ad ),
so this tests the exact same code path as production.
.PP
By default, delay and decoration ops (highlight, dim, fold, sign,
marker, snapshot, glide) are stripped from the op stream before
passing to the animator. Use
.B --show-delays
or
.B --show-decorations
to keep them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR --help ", " -h
Show help and exit.
.TP
.B --once
Display once and exit (no watching). Useful for testing.
.TP
.B --show-delays
Keep delay ops in the stream (default: stripped).
.TP
.B --show-decorations
Keep highlight/dim/fold/sign/marker ops (default: stripped).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Basic usage:
.B ad_watch old.py new.py ops.tsv
.TP
One-shot display (for testing):
.B ad_watch --once old.py new.py ops.tsv
.TP
Keep delay ops:
.B ad_watch --show-delays old.py new.py ops.tsv
.SH WORKFLOW
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# Terminal 1: generate initial ops, then watch
ad_gen_ops old.py new.py --ad-layer=ad_layer_reorder > /tmp/ops.tsv
ad_watch old.py new.py /tmp/ops.tsv

# Terminal 2: edit ops with syntax highlighting
vim -S scripts/vim/ad_ops_syntax.vim /tmp/ops.tsv
# :w to save \(em terminal 1 refreshes instantly
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ad_gen_ops (1),
.BR ad_tmux_watch (1),
.BR ad (1)
.PP
.I scripts/vim/ad_ops_syntax.vim
\(em vim syntax highlighting for op files
.PP
.I docs/design/AD_WATCH_REQUIREMENTS.md
\(em full requirements
